[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Advanced Kotlin Generics, Variance, and Type Erasure in Android"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generics are a cornerstone of modern Android development, enabling code reusability while ensuring type safety at compile time. However, mastering generics requires understanding variance (in, out, invariant) and how Kotlin handles generic types under the hood via type erasure."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Invariance: The Default State"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, generic classes in Kotlin are invariant. This means that if you have a class Box<T>, a Box<Dog> is not a subtype of Box<Animal>, even if Dog is a subtype of Animal. Invariance prevents type safety issues by restricting both reading and writing."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"open class Animal
class Dog : Animal()

class Box<T>(var item: T)

// Usage:
val animalBox: Box<Animal> = Box(Animal())
// val dogBox: Box<Animal> = Box<Dog>(Dog()) // Error: Type mismatch"},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Covariance with 'out' (Producer)"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"When you want a generic class to only produce (return) values of type T, you use the 'out' modifier. This is known as covariance. It allows you to pass a subtype where a supertype is expected. A classic Android example is List<out T>."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"interface Producer<out T> {
    fun produce(): T
}

// Usage:
val dogProducer: Producer<Dog> = object : Producer<Dog> {
    override fun produce(): Dog = Dog()
}

val animalProducer: Producer<Animal> = dogProducer // Perfectly valid due to 'out'"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Contravariance with 'in' (Consumer)"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Conversely, if your class only consumes (accepts as arguments) values of type T, you use the 'in' modifier. This is contravariance. It allows you to pass a supertype where a subtype is expected. Think of a Comparator<T>."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"interface Consumer<in T> {
    fun consume(item: T)
}

// Usage:
val animalConsumer: Consumer<Animal> = object : Consumer<Animal> {
    override fun consume(item: Animal) {}
}

val dogConsumer: Consumer<Dog> = animalConsumer // Valid due to 'in'"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Type Erasure in Kotlin/JVM"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"At runtime, Kotlin (like Java) does not retain the specific type arguments of generic classes. A List<String> and a List<Int> both become just List at runtime. This process is called type erasure."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"You cannot perform 'is' checks on specific generic types (e.g., if (list is List<String>) is a compilation error)."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Star projections (list is List<*>) must be used when checking generic instances at runtime."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Reified Type Parameters (The Solution for Android)"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Kotlin provides inline functions with 'reified' type parameters to bypass type erasure limitations. In Android development, this is extremely useful for starting Activities or parsing JSON."},{"order":18,"type":"code","text":"inline fun <reified T> startActivity(context: Context) {
    val intent = Intent(context, T::class.java)
    context.startActivity(intent)
}

// Usage in an Activity or Fragment:
startActivity<DetailActivity>(context)"}]
